+# Pre-ship Review — USDC Payouts dApp (Base)
+
+Scope: frontend (`packages/nextjs`) + its configuration. Contract is deployed
+and out of scope except where the frontend interacts with it. Report only — no
+code changed.
+
+Legend: **ship-blocking** = must fix before real users touch it; **should-fix** =
+fix this week, degrades UX/robustness but not a hard blocker.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Event feed scans from block 0 — the "Latest payouts" list will never load
+**Severity: ship-blocking**
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:32`
+
+```ts
+const { data: payouts } = useScaffoldEventHistory({
+  contractName: "Payouts",
+  eventName: "Paid",
+  fromBlock: 0n,   // <-- problem
+  watch: true,
+});
+```
+
+`useScaffoldEventHistory` backfills in **500-block batches, one sequential
+`getLogs` call at a time** (see `hooks/scaffold-eth/useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:183`
++ the `fetchNextPage` effect at `:257`). Base is past block ~33.7M, so starting at
+block 0 means **~67,000 sequential RPC round-trips** before the loop even reaches
+the contract's first block. Consequences:
+
+- The feed shows "No payouts yet." for effectively forever.
+- `watch: true` polling only starts *after* backfill finishes
+  (`shouldStartPolling` requires `!hasNextPage`, `:212`), so live updates never
+  kick in either.
+- 67k calls hammer the shared Alchemy key (see issue #7) → rate-limited → the
+  backfill stalls with errors.
+
+The hook already defaults `fromBlock` to the contract's `deployedOnBlock`
+(`33712450`, present in `deployedContracts.ts:48`). Passing `0n` explicitly
+overrides that sensible default.
+
+**Fix:** remove the `fromBlock: 0n` line entirely (defaults to `deployedOnBlock`),
+or set `fromBlock: 33712450n`. Note the hook itself is `@deprecated` for L2
+production use; for a shipping product the durable fix is an indexer (Ponder /
+subgraph), but removing `fromBlock: 0n` unblocks the ship.
+
+---
+
+## 2. No target-network guard — approve/pay can be sent on the wrong chain
+**Severity: ship-blocking**
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:21,36-62`
+
+The page uses raw wagmi `useWriteContract` and calls it with hardcoded Base
+addresses, without checking the wallet's connected chain:
+
+```ts
+const { writeContractAsync, isPending } = useWriteContract();
+// ...
+await writeContractAsync({ address: USDC.address, abi: USDC.abi, functionName: "approve", ... });
+```
+
+`writeContractAsync` submits to whatever chain the wallet is currently on. If a
+user is connected to Ethereum mainnet (or any non-Base chain), the app will send
+an `approve`/`pay` to the Base contract addresses **on the wrong network** — the
+Base USDC address `0x8335…2913` is a different contract on mainnet, and the
+Payouts address may not exist there at all. For a money-moving app this can burn
+gas on a bogus approval or worse.
+
+`useScaffoldWriteContract` handles this for free (enforces/switches to the target
+network and integrates notifications + the transactor).
+
+**Fix:** switch both writes to `useScaffoldWriteContract` (per AGENTS.md, "Always
+use hooks from `hooks/scaffold-eth`"), OR add an explicit chain guard: read
+`useTargetNetwork()` + `useAccount().chainId`, and if they differ, block the
+buttons and prompt a network switch (`useSwitchChain`).
+
+---
+
+## 3. All errors are swallowed — a failed payout gives the user zero feedback
+**Severity: ship-blocking**
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:44-46,59-61`
+
+```ts
+} catch (e) {
+  console.error(e);   // only feedback is a console log
+}
+```
+
+Both `handleApprove` and `handlePay` swallow every failure into `console.error`.
+Users see nothing — not on a reverted tx, not on rejected signature, not on a bad
+amount/address. In a payouts app this is dangerous: a user who doesn't realize the
+pay failed may retry and double-send, or assume a payment went through that
+didn't. There is also **no success feedback** — `handlePay` clears the form on
+success (`:57-58`) which looks identical to a silent failure.
+
+AGENTS.md explicitly requires `notification` from `~~/utils/scaffold-eth` for
+success/error and `getParsedError` for readable messages.
+
+**Fix:** in each `catch`, `notification.error(getParsedError(e))`; on success,
+`notification.success("Payout sent")`. (Adopting `useScaffoldWriteContract` per
+issue #2 wires the transactor's tx-status notifications automatically.)
+
+---
+
+## 4. Recipient address is never validated
+**Severity: should-fix**
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:78-84,55`
+
+The recipient is a plain `<input type="text">` and is cast with a lie to the type
+system before being passed to the contract:
+
+```ts
+args: [recipient as `0x${string}`, parseUnits(amount || "0", 6), memo],
+```
+
+A typo'd or non-address string reaches viem, which throws — and per issue #3 that
+throw is swallowed silently. No length/checksum validation, no ENS support.
+
+**Fix:** use `AddressInput` from `@scaffold-ui/components` (AGENTS.md: "Always use
+`@scaffold-ui/components`"), or at minimum gate the Send button with
+`isAddress(recipient)` from viem and show inline validation.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Amount input accepts values that silently fail; no validation/guardrails
+**Severity: should-fix**
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:85-91,42,55`
+
+`parseUnits(amount || "0", 6)` throws on more than 6 decimals (e.g. a pasted
+`1.2345678`), on scientific notation, etc. — again swallowed silently (#3). There
+is also no check that `amount > 0`, no check against the displayed USDC balance,
+and the Send button stays enabled with an empty/zero amount (contract will revert
+`"Payouts: zero amount"` with no user-facing reason).
+
+**Fix:** use `IntegerInput`/`EtherInput`-style validated input or clamp to 6
+decimals; disable Approve/Send when `amount` is empty/≤0 or exceeds balance; show
+a clear message instead of relying on a contract revert.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Single shared `isPending` couples the two buttons
+**Severity: should-fix**
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:21,100-113`
+
+`isPending` from one `useWriteContract` instance drives both buttons, so while an
+Approve is in flight the Send button also shows a spinner and is disabled, and
+vice-versa — the UI can't tell the user which action is actually running. There is
+also no allowance read, so the two-step Approve → Send flow is entirely manual
+with no indication of whether an approval is even needed.
+
+**Fix:** track pending state per action (or use two `useScaffoldWriteContract`
+instances). Optionally read `allowance(owner, Payouts)` and hide/disable Approve
+when the current allowance already covers the amount.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Production relies on the shared default Alchemy key & WalletConnect ID
+**Severity: should-fix**
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts:16,29,40`
+
+```ts
+export const DEFAULT_ALCHEMY_API_KEY = "IZYEU2cWBgnFmgiTAgpWD";
+// ...
+walletConnectProjectId: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID || '3a8170812b534d0ff9d794f19a901d64',
+```
+
+These are the shared Scaffold-ETH default keys. Shipping to real users on Base
+means every session shares one heavily-rate-limited Alchemy key — combined with
+issue #1's request storm, RPC throttling is likely, causing reads/feed to fail
+intermittently. The WalletConnect default project ID is similarly not yours
+(analytics/allowlist not under your control).
+
+**Fix:** provision your own Alchemy app + WalletConnect project, set
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID` in the
+Vercel/system env (`.env.example` already lists both). Verify they're populated in
+the production environment before shipping.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Polling interval is high for an L2 payouts feed
+**Severity: should-fix**
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts:22`
+
+`pollingInterval: 30000` (30s). AGENTS.md: "Remember to decrease the polling
+interval for L2 chains." Base has ~2s blocks; a 30s interval means balance updates
+and new payouts in the feed lag up to 30 seconds, which feels broken for a
+real-time payout confirmation flow.
+
+**Fix:** lower to a few seconds (e.g. `pollingInterval: 5000`), balancing
+freshness against RPC quota (tie to the dedicated key from #7).
+
+---
+
+## 9. List rendered with array-index keys
+**Severity: should-fix (minor)**
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:120-121`
+
+```tsx
+{(payouts ?? []).map((payout, i) => (
+  <div key={i} ...>
+```
+
+Live events are **prepended** to the array as they arrive (`liveEvents` +
+polling), so index keys don't correspond to stable rows — React may mis-reconcile
+list items as new payouts land.
+
+**Fix:** key on a stable identifier, e.g.
+``key={`${payout.transactionHash}-${payout.logIndex}`}``.
+
+---
+
